<BaseLayout title="BL40 (Weizhong) — Bend Middle Acupressure Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate BL40 (Weizhong) — the Bend Middle point. Benefits include back pain relief, sciatica support, and knee pain relief.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL40 (Weizhong) — Bend Middle</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL40, known as Weizhong or Bend Middle, is located at the back of the knee and is a major point for acute lower back pain, sciatica, and knee discomfort.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl40-location.png" alt="BL40 acupressure point location behind the knee" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">BL40 is at the back of the knee, in the exact center of the popliteal crease (the horizontal skin crease behind the knee joint). It lies on the Bladder meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Lower back pain:</strong> BL40 is considered one of the most effective points for acute lower back strain and discomfort.</li>
      <li><strong>Sciatica:</strong> It helps relieve radiating leg pain caused by sciatic nerve compression.</li>
      <li><strong>Knee pain:</strong> BL40 eases stiffness and pain in the knee joint, especially from overuse or arthritis.</li>
      <li><strong>Leg cramps:</strong> It relaxes tight hamstrings and calf muscles and relieves leg cramps.</li>
      <li><strong>Bladder health:</strong> BL40 can help with urinary issues and bladder infections in traditional use.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage BL40</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit with your knee slightly bent and leg relaxed.</li>
      <li>Place your thumbs on the BL40 point behind your knee.</li>
      <li>Apply firm, steady pressure with a small circular motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds, then gently straighten and bend your leg.</li>
      <li>Repeat on the other leg as needed.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2017 systematic review in <em>Journal of Chinese Medicine Research</em> identified BL40 as one of the most commonly selected points for treating lower back pain in clinical trials.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27111349/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Acupuncture in Medicine</em> found that BL40 stimulation reduced pain and improved function in patients with chronic low back pain.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25388950/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2020 study in <em>Journal of Pain Research</em> showed that deep pressure on BL40 increased local blood circulation and reduced muscle tension in the lumbar region.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32982404/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid deep pressure if you have a knee injury, Baker's cyst, or recent knee surgery.</li>
      <li>Do not massage over varicose veins or deep vein thrombosis if present behind the knee.</li>
      <li>Use gentle pressure on the elderly or those with fragile skin.</li>
      <li>Stop if you experience sharp or shooting pain down the leg.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">BL40 works well with <a href="/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/">BL60 (Kunlun)</a> for sciatica and leg pain. For lower back pain, combine with <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point/">CV6 (Qihai)</a> and <a href="/points/kd3-taixi-acupressure-point/">KD3 (Taixi)</a> for kidney support.</p>
  </section></article>
